  :adr/body "# ADR 2799007125: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7125 Glaziers job-site scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7125\n-- Glaziers** only, Wave3 (production/trades) of the rollout (Wave3\nbatch1 of 6 already landed: 7111/7112/7113/7114/7119/7121).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7125\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"7125\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Glaziers\" -- confirming the\npremise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's `:maturity` was\n`:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics :identity\n:audit-ledger]`, `:optional-technologies []` and `:operating-states\n[:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit]`, and no\n`:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7125` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the\n`7125` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nGlaziers install glass/glazing on active job sites -- real\nphysical-safety stakes (glass-handling cuts, height-work for building\nfacades, heavy-panel handling). The controlling constraint for this\nvertical is that the closed op allowlist must NEVER include any op\nthat directly finalizes a glazing-installation-execution decision, or\noverrides a site safety officer's judgment -- those decisions must\nalways be a hard permanent block or an always-escalate op, never\nauto-commit-eligible. This actor is deliberately scoped to JOB-SITE\nSCHEDULING/LOGISTICS COORDINATION ONLY -- it never performs glazing\nwork itself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored closely for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111)\n(House Builders: same wave/domain shape, `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/\n`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set: README,\nCODE_OF_CONDUCT.md, CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, LICENSE\n(AGPL-3.0-or-later), SECURITY.md, docs/business-model.md,\ndocs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn) -- cloned and read in full in\nthis session, just landed at the time of this ADR.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7125` as an OSS job-site\nscheduling/logistics-coordination actor for glaziers, following the\nSAME governed-actor architecture as every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*`\nactor in this fleet: **Glazier Advisor ⊣ GlazierGovernor** on a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`,\ngit-sha-pinned identically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- a\ndifferent library coordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the\nunrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger. All source is portable `.cljc` (no JVM interop); tests\nare `clojure.test` `.clj`, matching the reference repo's own layout.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **job-site scheduling/logistics coordination\nONLY, never direct glazing-installation-execution authority** -- it\nmust NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose\nexercising a glazing-installation-execution decision, or any override\nof a site safety officer's judgment. These are not merely gated behind\nescalation -- they are structurally absent from the op-allowlist\nentirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- task/materials-usage/progress data logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a glass-handling/height-work/\n  panel-hazard concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- glazing-materials procurement proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** a\nglazing-installation-execution decision or a site-safety-officer-\njudgment override. These are **structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind escalation -- an op\nsuch as `:finalize-glazing-installation-decision` cannot even be\nproposed by the reference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for`\nfunctions only ever emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any\ncode path (a forged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving\nfork) ever produced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD\ncheck (`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently. A\nsecond, independent HARD check (`:scope-excluded-action`) names five\nconcretely-forbidden ops (`glazier.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-glazing-installation-decision`,\n`:authorize-panel-installation`, `:proceed-with-glazing-installation`,\n`:override-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`:override-site-safety-officer-judgment`) as defense-in-depth, and is\nverified by dedicated unit tests\n(`glazier.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-glazing-installation-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-panel-installation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-glazing-installation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-site-safety-officer-judgment`).\n\n### Five HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Glazier unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered glazing crew member record\n   (`:no-glazier`). Never trusts the proposal's own claim of who the\n   glazier is.\n2. **Site unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an independently\n   registered job site (`:no-site`). Together with check 1, this\n   directly implements the CRITICAL invariant that a worker/site\n   record must be independently verified/registered before any\n   action.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)\n   (`:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op\n   allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`); the closed\n   op-allowlist is enforced unconditionally.\n5. **Scope-excluded action** -- any proposal whose `:op` names one of\n   the five concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text\n   `:rationale` names a finalization/execution action for proceeding\n   with the glazing installation, or overriding a site safety\n   officer's judgment, is a second HARD, PERMANENT block\n   (`:scope-excluded-action`), evaluated unconditionally on content --\n   defense-in-depth on top of the allowlist check: even if a future\n   change somehow widened the allowlist, content-based rejection still\n   independently blocks glazing-installation-execution/safety-override\n   language wherever it appears in free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a glass-handling/\n  height-work/panel-hazard observation -- it never resolves the\n  concern itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is the hazard\n  data,\" never \"here is what I recommend doing about site safety.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an over-threshold\n  glazing-materials order is not itself unsafe, unlike a\n  glazing-installation-execution or safety-officer-override attempt,\n  which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft escalate\n  vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"glass\", \"panel\", \"safety\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly the risk\nthis vertical's own vocabulary (a job-site coordination actor working\nadjacent to glazing-installation work necessarily discusses glass and\npanel concepts to do its scheduling/logging job) could trigger if\nphrased carelessly. `glazier.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is\nphrased exclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"proceed\nwith the glazing installation\", \"authorize the panel installation\",\n\"override the site safety officer's judgment\" -- matching the task's\nown worked example phrasing), never bare nouns, and\n`glazier.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale\ntemplate was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (\"logged\nwork record for glazier ...\", \"scheduled crew operation for glazing\ninstallation task ...\", \"... routed for site safety officer review\")\nWITHOUT containing any of the full finalization-action phrases, so the\nregression test is a genuine stress test of the bare-noun-vs-action-\nphrase distinction, not a vacuous pass. Green on first run in this\nsession -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never\ntripped during development.\n`glazier.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`\nadditionally exercises the same invariant through the full graph run,\nconfirming a scope-excluded proposal never reaches `commit-record!`\nregardless of disposition path.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7125\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7125` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7125`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit to account for concurrent sibling landings in this\n  same batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 22 tests, 47 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- Full doc set published: README.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,\n  CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, SECURITY.md, LICENSE\n  (AGPL-3.0-or-later, byte-identical to the `cloud-itonami-isco-7111`\n  reference's LICENSE text), docs/business-model.md,\n  docs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-glazing-installation` that is\n  merely gated behind an always-escalate list (the shape\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for `:approve-over-ceiling-posting`):\n  rejected outright per the task's explicit domain-design guidance --\n  an always-escalate op still means the *capability* to finalize a\n  glazing-installation-execution decision exists in the code path and\n  is merely deferred to a human gate. For a decision this severe\n  (glass-handling cuts, height-work falls, heavy-panel handling\n  injury), the capability itself must not exist. The only op related\n  to site-safety escalation is `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces\n  the need for human/site-safety-officer attention and never itself\n  decides, authorizes, or defers a glazing-installation decision for\n  later automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring the 3313\n  reference's transaction-amount-ceiling HARD check): rejected per the\n  task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold glazing-materials\n  order is not itself unsafe, so it is handled as a soft escalate\n  gate, not a hard block; only the glazing-installation-execution/\n  safety-officer-override class is a hard, unconditional block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"glass\" \"panel\"\n  \"safety\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above (and CLAUDE.md's own worked example for this\n  exact task), and per the explicit instruction to phrase\n  scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS, never bare\n  nouns -- this actor's own domain necessarily discusses\n  glass/panel/safety-officer concepts to do its scheduling/logging\n  job, so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking the actor's own\n  legitimate proposals (regression-tested directly against rationale\n  text that deliberately contains those bare nouns).\n- Merging `glazier` and `site` into a single entity (only checking one\n  provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape\n  (independently-registered glazier AND independently-registered site)\n  matches the CRITICAL invariant that a \"worker/site record must be\n  independently verified/registered before any action\" and the\n  established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors with\n  two-sided provenance checks.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- task/materials-usage/progress data\n   logging only. Requires an independently registered glazier AND\n   site (`:no-glazier`/`:no-site`). Not a\n   glazing-installation-execution or safety-officer decision in any\n   respect -- it is a records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n   only. Cannot carry a glazing-installation-execution decision\n   (structurally absent from the op's field shape; any such attempt\n   via free text is caught by the `:scope-excluded-action` content\n   check). Does not authorize, finalize, or draw any conclusion about\n   a specific glass-panel installation -- it is a calendar/logistics\n   action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces a glass-handling/\n   height-work/panel-hazard concern to a human/site safety officer;\n   ALWAYS escalates, unconditionally, at every confidence level, NEVER\n   auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY channel through which a\n   safety observation may be surfaced, and it explicitly never\n   resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself -- the site safety\n   officer reviews and acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- glazing-materials procurement\n   proposal only (item, cost). Not a glazing-installation-execution or\n   authorization decision of any kind -- it is a purchasing-logistics\n   action, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling escalation (above 2000\n   always escalates).\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na glazing-installation-execution decision (e.g. proceeding with a\nspecific glass-panel installation) or a site-safety-officer-judgment\noverride -- these categories are structurally absent, not gated,\nconfirmed by: (a) the closed four-op allowlist itself\n(`glazier.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) five concretely-named\nforbidden-op fixtures rejected at the governor-unit level\n(`glazier.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`glazier.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-glazing-installation-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-panel-installation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-glazing-installation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-site-safety-officer-judgment`),\nand (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`glazier.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,\n`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against a rationale\nthat attempts to proceed with the glazing installation or override a\nsite safety officer's judgment even via an otherwise-allowlisted op\n(`glazier.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-even-with-allowlisted-op`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-site-safety-officer`).\n"}]
